  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
     CLIENTS — the engagement a piece of work belongs to

     AIMY-1253 asks for retrieval by client and the Lead Tracking map puts
     Clients in the permission list. Both need the same object, which did not
     exist: the README declared retrieval-by-client "not built" because there
     was no client model to filter on.

     A client is an organisation FlairsTech runs an engagement for. Most
     campaigns here are FlairsTech's own book and carry `client: null` — that
     is not a missing value, it is the commonest case, and forcing every
     campaign to name a client would have invented an owner for work that
     genuinely has none.

     NOTHING STORES A CLIENT ON A RECORD. A lead reaches a client the way it
     reaches a campaign: through membership, derived in `reindex()` into
     `DB.clientsOf`. A lead can sit in two engagements, and the second copy
     of that fact is the one that would drift.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* THREE FIELDS, NOT SIX. `since` (when the engagement started), `owner`
     (who at FlairsTech holds it) and `svc` (which services it buys) were all
     written here and read nowhere.

     No requirement asks for any of them. R2.2 says what a client SEES — name,
     goal, analytics, where we are now — and none of that is about the
     engagement itself. Ownership in the brief is per-campaign (R2.3), which
     is `camp.owner` and works. `svc` was the tempting one: R2.4 is the
     brief's named problem, "they don't know what they are selling properly",
     and an engagement's services could seed a campaign's `sells` — but
     nobody asked for that, `camp.sells` already answers it per campaign, and
     inventing the seam now would be modelling ahead of the brief.

     `what` stays. It is read: the create form's client picker renders its
     first clause under each name, so "Upland" arrives with "Education
     software" attached for anyone who does not know the account. v5 removed
     it from the campaign EDIT dialog, where two sentences per option on a
     three-option pick was noise; one clause introducing a name is not. */
  /* `fee` is what the engagement bills — MOCK, like every other price in
     this build. It is here rather than derived because it is not derivable:
     what a client pays is a contract, not a consequence of the work.

     It is also the whole of what a client's page counts. Their cost per
     meeting is their fee over their meetings, never ours over ours — our
     unit costs, our suppliers and our margin do not cross this boundary,
     and the client page is built so they have nowhere to leak from. */
  /* `promised` IS THE CLIENT'S TARGET, and it is what lets their page be
     the same page as the CEO's.

     A client has no revenue quota with us — what they bought is delivery.
     So the yardstick is the number of meetings the contract commits us to
     in a quarter, and every question the executive page asks becomes
     answerable for them too: how many of the promised meetings have landed,
     how much of the quarter is gone, and how many more AiMY expects. Mock,
     per quarter, like the fee beside it. */
  const CLIENTS = [
    { k: 'upland',  name: 'Upland',
      what: 'Education software. We prospect their EMEA pilot market and run the first two touches.',
      fee: { monthly: 14500, perMeeting: 850 }, promised: 12 },
    { k: 'kestrel', name: 'Kestrel Voice',
      what: 'Contact-centre platform. Their EMEA financial-services push, sourced and qualified here.',
      fee: { monthly: 18500, perMeeting: 950 }, promised: 15 },
  ];
  const CLIENT_BY = Object.create(null);
  CLIENTS.forEach((c) => (CLIENT_BY[c.k] = c));
  const clientName = (k) => (CLIENT_BY[k] ? CLIENT_BY[k].name : k);

